export const lessons=[
 {id:'board',unit:1,title:'Small board, big jobs',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'MEET',intro:'The ESP32 runs the program you write and talks to the outside world through its pins.',question:'Which connection do you use to switch an LED on and off from your program?',choices:['GPIO: a pin your program controls','GND: common ground','3V3: fixed supply'],answer:0,explanation:'A GPIO can be an input or an output. Set as an output, your program can switch the LED on and off. GND is the common reference and 3V3 is a supply.',hint:'We’re looking for an output the program can change.'},
 {id:'serial',unit:1,title:'First message from the board',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'READ',intro:'The Serial Monitor shows the messages the board sends you. On a real board, pick the same baud rate in the code and in the monitor.',code:'void setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  Serial.println("Hello, ESP32!");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n}',question:'What does this program print in the Serial Monitor?',choices:['Hello, ESP32! — once','Hello, ESP32! — over and over','115200 — once'],answer:0,explanation:'setup() runs once when the board starts. loop() repeats, but here it is empty. 115200 is not a message; it is the serial speed.',hint:'Is the message inside setup() or inside loop()?'},
 {id:'setup',unit:1,title:'The rhythm of a program',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'PREDICT',intro:'Reading a program is a good way to start writing one. This time the message is inside loop().',code:'void setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println("One more step");\n  delay(1000);\n}',question:'Roughly how often is the message repeated?',choices:['Once a second','Once a millisecond','Only when the board starts'],answer:0,explanation:'delay() waits in milliseconds. 1000 ms = 1 second. Then loop() runs again.',hint:'How many seconds are 1000 milliseconds?'},
 {id:'rows',unit:2,title:'Hidden connections',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'EXPLORE',intro:'Inside the breadboard are metal strips. Holes a–e with the same number form one group; holes f–j form another.',question:'A wire in hole a7 is directly connected to which hole?',choices:['e7','f7','a8'],answer:0,explanation:'a7, b7, c7, d7 and e7 are connected inside. Because of the gap in the middle, f7 is in a separate group. The neighbouring column a8 is separate too.',hint:'Keep the number the same and don’t cross the middle gap.'},
 {id:'resistor',unit:2,title:'A small part with a big job',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'UNDERSTAND',intro:'A resistor in series with the LED limits the current. Let’s assume about 2 V for a red LED and 3.3 V for the GPIO.',question:'About how much current flows through the 220 Ω resistor?',choices:['6 mA: (3.3 − 2) / 220','15 A: 3.3 − 220','0 mA: the resistor blocks all current'],answer:0,explanation:'About 1.3 V is left across the resistor. 1.3 V / 220 Ω ≈ 0.0059 A, or 5.9 mA. In this simplified model we take the LED’s forward voltage as 2 V.',hint:'Ohm’s law: current = voltage across the resistor / resistance.'},
 {id:'polarity',unit:2,title:'Two ends of a circuit',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'CONNECT',intro:'An LED has a direction. The long leg is the anode (+), the short leg is the cathode (−). We need a closed path for the current.',question:'Which of these is a correct start?',choices:['GPIO 23 → resistor → LED + → LED − → GND','GPIO 23 → LED − → LED + → resistor → GND','GPIO 23 → resistor → LED +; LED − not connected'],answer:0,explanation:'The current flows from the GPIO through the resistor and the LED back to GND. A reversed LED stays dark in this model, and with one leg left free the circuit is not closed.',hint:'Check both the direction and the way back to GND.'},
 {id:'light',unit:3,title:'Light your first LED',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'lab',mode:'steady',tag:'BUILD',intro:'Your turn. Place the resistor and the LED on the breadboard. Build a path from GPIO 23 through the resistor to the LED’s + leg, and from its − leg to GND.',hint:'One example: resistor a4–a7, LED a7–a8. Connect GPIO 23 → c4 and c8 → GND. Holes a–e in the same column are connected.'},
 {id:'blink',unit:3,title:'Give the light a rhythm',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'lab',mode:'blink',tag:'PROGRAM',intro:'Same circuit, new behaviour. Change the delay and watch the LED’s rhythm. One full blink takes two delays.',hint:'500 ms on + 500 ms off = a 1-second cycle. The wiring is the same as in the first task.'},
 {id:'challenge',unit:3,title:'Your turn: blink twice',minutes:5,xp:60,type:'lab',mode:'challenge',tag:'CHALLENGE',intro:'Goal: the LED completes two full on-off cycles every second. Build the circuit and pick the right value for both delay() calls.',hint:'One cycle has two parts, on and off. Two cycles mean four equal delays.'},
 {id:'pullup',unit:4,title:'What does the button say?',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'MEET',intro:'A button has four legs, but inside it is just a simple switch. The two left legs are always connected to each other, and so are the two right legs. Pressing it connects left to right. In this unit the button sits between GPIO 22 and GND.',code:'void setup() {\n  pinMode(22, INPUT_PULLUP);\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(digitalRead(22));\n  delay(50);\n}',question:'What does the Serial Monitor show while the button is not pressed?',choices:['1 (HIGH): the internal pull-up pulls the pin to 3.3 V','0 (LOW): the button is connected to GND','Random 0s and 1s: the pin is floating'],answer:0,explanation:'INPUT_PULLUP switches on a resistor of about 45 kΩ inside the ESP32 and pulls the pin HIGH. When pressed, the button connects the pin straight to GND. The button beats the weak resistor and the reading becomes LOW, or 0.',hint:'A pull-up pulls the pin up, to 3.3 V.'},
 {id:'floating',unit:4,title:'The floating pin',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'UNDERSTAND',intro:'A pin connected to nothing is “floating”. It picks up noise from the air, even from your hand nearby.',code:'void setup() {\n  pinMode(22, INPUT);   // no pull-up\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(digitalRead(22));\n  delay(50);\n}',question:'Same circuit: what do you see while the button is not pressed?',choices:['Random jumping 0s and 1s','Always 1','Always 0'],answer:0,explanation:'In INPUT mode the internal resistor is off. A released button connects the pin to nothing, so the reading changes at random. INPUT_PULLUP calms the pin down. The other option is a 10 kΩ pull-down resistor from the pin to GND with the button wired to 3.3 V; then a press reads HIGH.',hint:'What is connected to the pin while the button is not pressed?'},
 {id:'button',unit:4,title:'A button lights an LED',minutes:6,xp:40,type:'lab',mode:'button',tag:'BUILD',intro:'Your LED circuit is ready. Place the button across the gap in the middle of the breadboard. Connect GPIO 22 to one side of the button and the other side to GND. Then run the circuit, press the button and let go.',hint:'The button sits in rows e and f. Example: put it in column 10 (10–12), then connect GPIO 22 → h10 and h12 → GND. If the wire and GND end up on the same side of the button, the pin always reads LOW.'},
 {id:'bounce',unit:4,title:'One press, one count',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'bounce',tag:'MEASURE',intro:'When metal contacts touch, they bounce for a few milliseconds. The ESP32 reads the pin so fast that one press can look like several. After each change, ignore new changes for a short time: this is called debouncing.',hint:'The bounces here last less than 20 ms. The video uses 50 ms; leaving some margin is good.'},
 {id:'millis',unit:4,title:'Check the clock, don’t wait',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'CALCULATE',intro:'millis() returns the milliseconds since the board started, and it counts by itself. Instead of waiting, we check the clock every time round the loop: have 500 ms passed since the last change?',code:'unsigned long now = millis();\nif (now - lastBlink >= 500) {\n  lastBlink = now;\n  ledOn = !ledOn;\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledOn ? HIGH : LOW);\n}',question:'lastBlink is 700 and now is 1000. What happens in this loop?',choices:['Nothing: 1000 − 700 = 300, not 500 yet','The LED changes: 1000 is greater than 500','The program waits 200 ms'],answer:0,explanation:'We always subtract: now − lastBlink. 300 < 500, so the condition is false and loop() moves on without waiting. When now reaches 1200, 500 ms have passed: the LED changes and lastBlink becomes 1200. Subtraction even works when millis() rolls over to 0 after about 49.7 days.',hint:'Work out the difference in the condition: now − lastBlink.'},
 {id:'nowait',unit:4,title:'Two things at once',minutes:6,xp:60,type:'nowait',tag:'CHALLENGE',intro:'We want to listen to the button while the LED blinks. Each press stops the blinking or starts it again. First press the button briefly a few times in the delay() version. Then switch to the millis() version and catch three presses in a row without missing one.',hint:'While delay() waits, the ESP32 doesn’t look at the button at all; it is only read between the waits. In the millis() version, loop() never waits.'}
];
